# GitHub Actions workflows this fork runs. Every other workflow is disabled.
#
# The upstream workflows stay in the tree, unedited, so an upstream sync merges
# without touching them; they are switched off in the repository's Actions
# state instead, by .github/compliance/enforce-workflow-allowlist.sh. That
# state is keyed by workflow path and survives syncs. A workflow a sync ADDS is
# enabled by default, which is why the enforcer runs on every push to master
# that touches .github/workflows/.
#
# Why the upstream lanes are off here, not merely noisy:
#  - ci.yml / ci-master.yml queue jobs on upstream self-hosted runners
#    (dsh-win-ci, vm-backup) that do not exist in this organization; each such
#    job waits 24 hours for its timeout.
#  - e2e.yml, sandbox.yml, pi-ai-provider-e2e.yml need upstream API keys.
#  - release*.yml, python-release.yml, node-addon-system-release.yml and
#    build-exe-for-python-sdk.yml publish the upstream npm/PyPI packages.
#  - issue-*.yml, weighted-approval*.yml and build-preview-cloudflare.yml
#    operate the upstream's GitHub App, project board and Cloudflare account.
#
# What still guards the harness itself after a sync: compliance-plugins.yml
# runs `pnpm run build` and the plugin smokes, and docker-image.yml builds the
# image and boots it.
#
# One path per line, relative to the repository root. The enforcer refuses to
# run if a listed path does not exist in the tree, so a typo or a rename fails
# loudly instead of silently leaving a lane off.

.github/workflows/compliance-plugins.yml
.github/workflows/docker-image.yml
.github/workflows/helm-chart.yml
.github/workflows/compliance-workflow-allowlist.yml
